{"author":{"id":"f21006d1826991d76b358f177209294474e8c2701fc659ff8fe8b8f40ee77f55"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1747150600,"nonce":"FURD/MJFklQdCHdEsQlzEqv9o0w=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOCGKeh86re553","github-url":"https://github.com/git-bug/git-bug/issues/1416#issuecomment-2877005431"},"message":"thanks for getting back to me.\n\n\u003e I can push and pull using git bug bridge push origin and git bug bridge pull origin respectively.\n\nyes -- as stated in my comment, `git-bug-bridge-{push,pull}` and `git-bug-{push,pull}` **behave differently**. the former uses your **bridge credentials** (e.g. your oauth token) to hit your bridge's API. the latter uses `git` (well, go-git) to effectively do a `git push` (of your git-bug entities).\n\n---\n\n\u003e This is my repo URL: https://github.com/JoacoG/suite-cupones.\n\u003e **It is private repo.**\n\nis this the **url for your git remote** (as in, the actual output from `git remote get-url origin` on the command line)? if so, this is the issue -- git-bug's `git` operations don't support authentication for `http(s)` remotes.\n\nnormally, with a remote like this, `git` prompts you for your username and password (or access token). IIRC github requires that you use a personal access token instead of your password, so your workflow would look something like this:\n\n```\n➜ git remote get-url origin\nhttps://github.com/sudoforge/test.git\n\n\n➜ git pull\nUsername for 'https://github.com': \u003cusername\u003e\nPassword for 'https://github.com': \u003cpersonal access token\u003e\n```\n\nbut git-bug doesn't currently \"know\" how to do this for sort check-first-and-then-prompt-the-user-for-creds. additionally, `go-git` (the library we use) doesn't support reading from `~/.netrc`, or the git credential chain.\n\nwe probably _should_, since git does, so i'll change this issue's title so that it represents the feature request more accurately.\n\n---\n\nin the meantime, you can solve this in a couple of different ways.\n\n- changing your git remote to `https://\u003cuser\u003e:\u003ctoken\u003e@github.com/foo/bar.git`\n- **RECOMMENDED**: use `ssh` instead; this is more secure and generally a better way to use git for private repositories (anywhere; not just for ones on github).","files":null},{"type":6,"timestamp":1747150666,"nonce":"t63vz2vEls3czGPOB6jQZRSVRzs=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lALOCGKeh86re553zmbJPbw"},"target":"4ce47b3c7ca2413220cff3785a0d85b66e0f53e7fbbef3cad619de66337766bb","message":"thanks for getting back to me.\n\n\u003e I can push and pull using git bug bridge push origin and git bug bridge pull origin respectively.\n\nyes -- as stated in my comment, `git-bug-bridge-{push,pull}` and `git-bug-{push,pull}` **behave differently**. the former uses your **bridge credentials** (e.g. your oauth token) to hit your bridge's API. the latter uses `git` (well, go-git) to effectively do a `git push` (of your git-bug entities).\n\n---\n\n\u003e This is my repo URL: https://github.com/JoacoG/suite-cupones.\n\u003e **It is private repo.**\n\nis this the **url for your git remote** (as in, the actual output from `git remote get-url origin` on the command line)? if so, this is the issue -- git-bug's `git` operations don't support authentication for `http(s)` remotes.\n\nnormally, with a remote like this, `git` prompts you for your username and password (or access token). IIRC github requires that you use a personal access token instead of your password, so your workflow would look something like this:\n\n```\n➜ git remote get-url origin\nhttps://github.com/sudoforge/test.git\n\n\n➜ git pull\nUsername for 'https://github.com': \u003cusername\u003e\nPassword for 'https://github.com': \u003cpersonal access token\u003e\n```\n\nbut git-bug doesn't currently \"know\" how to do this for sort check-first-and-then-prompt-the-user-for-creds. additionally, `go-git` (the library we use) doesn't support reading from `~/.netrc`, or any `credential.helper` configuration you've set up for git.\n\nwe probably _should_, since git does, so i'll change this issue's title so that it represents the feature request more accurately.\n\n---\n\nin the meantime, you can solve this in a couple of different ways.\n\n- changing your git remote to `https://\u003cuser\u003e:\u003ctoken\u003e@github.com/foo/bar.git`\n- **RECOMMENDED**: use `ssh` instead; this is more secure and generally a better way to use git for private repositories (anywhere; not just for ones on github).","files":null},{"type":2,"timestamp":1747150796,"nonce":"41ObMrW5g4lGBFkq708uvTw6quE=","metadata":{"github-id":"RTE_lADOCGKeh862UPb7zwAAAAQbUbCa"},"title":"push, pull should handle authenticated http(s) remotes","was":"git: support authenticated http(s) remotes"}]}